{
  "ID": "3.1",
  "Context/Background": "To find novel treatments and drug combinations that could effectively target colorectal cancer cells (CRCs) and lower the probability of disease relapse, we designed multiple different combinations between cisplatin, cannabidiol (CBD), and intermittent serum starvation (ISS) on colorectal cancer cell lines. We analyzed differentially expressed genes and affected pathways in colorectal cancer cell lines to understand further the potential molecular mechanisms behind the treatments and their interactions.",
  "Prompt": "Based on the results of the differential expression analysis of Cisplatin vs DMSO in colorectal cancer cells, run a pathway overrepresentation analysis for statistically significant genes (up and down combined) against the Reactome database. Then, provide a discussion of any relevant affected pathways, highlighting important up and down regulated genes in each.  Focus on any mechanisms by which Cisplatin may alter cancer related pathways.",
  "mode": "Discovera",
  "llm_provider": "nvidia",
  "llm_model": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b",
  "run_date": "2026-01-20 21:17:34",
  "elapsed_seconds": 118.28,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "### Introduction/Context\nThe analysis focuses on colorectal cancer (CRC) cell lines treated with **cisplatin** versus DMSO (control), using a differentially expressed gene (DEG) dataset. The goal is to identify Reactome pathways overrepresented in statistically significant genes (adjusted *p*-value < 0.05) to uncover mechanisms by which cisplatin alters cancer-related pathways. The dataset includes 1018 significant genes (padj < 0.05).\n\n---\n\n### Keywords and Candidate Genes\n**Keywords**: *cisplatin*, *TP53*, *cell cycle arrest*, *ER stress*, *apoptosis*.  \n**Candidate Genes**: *TP53*, *CDKN1A*, *CDKN1B*, *BAX*, *FAS*, *ATF4*, *NFE2L2*.\n\n---\n\n### Pathway Enrichments\n#### Reactome Pathways (Top Enriched Terms)\n| **Pathway** | **Direction** | **Leading Genes** | **P-Value** | **Adjusted P-Value** | **Odds Ratio** |\n|-------------|---------------|-------------------|-------------|-----------------------|----------------|\n| **TP53 Regulates Transcription of Cell Cycle Genes** | Up | *PLK3, BTG2, CDKN1A, CDKN1B, PCNA, GADD45A, CCNE2, BAX, SFN, ZNF385A, E2F7* | 7.30E-13 | 1.15E-9 | 12.25 |\n| **TP53 Regulates G2 Cell Cycle Arrest** | Up | *PCNA, GADD45A, PRMT1, BAX, EP300, SFN, ZNF385A* | 1.06E-6 | 0.00033 | 15.31 |\n| **ATF4 Activates Genes in Response to ER Stress** | Up | *DDIT3, ASNS, ATF6, ATF3, HERPUD1, ATF4* | 5.50E-6 | 0.00109 | 9.07 |\n| **TP53 Regulates Transcription of Cell Death Genes** | Up | *PIDD1, BNIP3L, TP53I3, TNFRSF10C, PMAIP1, BAX, FAS, TNFRSF10A* | 7.74E-6 | 0.00122 | 6.39 |\n| **NFE2L2 Regulating Tumorigenic Genes** | Up | *NOTCH1, SP1, EP300, BCL2L1* | 0.00155 | 0.0289 | 10.90 |\n| **CASP8 Activity Is Inhibited** | Up | *FAS, TNFRSF10B, TNFRSF10A, CFLAR* | 0.00155 | 0.0289 | 10.90 |\n\n---\n\n### Targeted (Keyword-Guided) Subset\n**TP53-Regulated Pathways**:  \n- **Cell Cycle Arrest** (*CDKN1A, CDKN1B, GADD45A, CCNE2*) and **Apoptosis** (*BAX, FAS, PIDD1*) dominate, reflecting cisplatin’s DNA damage-induced activation of TP53.  \n- **ER Stress Response** (*DDIT3, ATF4, HERPUD1*) suggests cisplatin triggers endoplasmic reticulum (ER) stress, linking DNA damage to proteotoxic stress.  \n- **NFE2L2 Pathway** (*BCL2L1, NOTCH1*) indicates activation of antioxidant responses, which may counteract cisplatin-induced oxidative stress and promote survival.\n\n---\n\n### Key Genes/Proteins (Leading Edge)\n| **Gene** | **Direction** | **Role/Function** | **Pathway(s)** |\n|----------|---------------|-------------------|----------------|\n| **CDKN1A** | Up | Cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitor; mediates G1/S arrest | TP53, Cell Cycle |\n| **CDKN1B** | Up | Inhibits G1 cyclin complexes; induces cell cycle arrest | TP53, Cell Cycle |\n| **BAX** | Up | Pro-apoptotic BCL-2 family member; mitochondrial apoptosis | TP53, Cell Death |\n| **FAS** | Up | Death receptor mediating extrinsic apoptosis | TP53, Cell Death |\n| **DDIT3** | Up | ER stress-induced apoptosis via CHOP | ATF4, ER Stress |\n| **ATF4** | Up | Transcription factor activating stress response genes | ATF4, ER Stress |\n| **BCL2L1** | Up | Anti-apoptotic; inhibits mitochondrial apoptosis | NFE2L2, Survival |\n\n---\n\n### Mechanistic Discussion\n1. **TP53 Activation**:  \n   Cisplatin induces DNA damage, activating **TP53**, which upregulates *CDKN1A* and *CDKN1B* to halt the cell cycle (G1/S and G2/M phases), allowing repair or triggering apoptosis via *BAX* and *FAS*. This aligns with cisplatin’s role as a DNA-damaging agent.\n\n2. **ER Stress and Apoptosis**:  \n   Enrichment of **ATF4** and **DDIT3** (CHOP) pathways indicates cisplatin induces ER stress. Prolonged ER stress activates CHOP, promoting apoptosis. This links DNA damage to proteotoxic stress, amplifying cell death signals.\n\n3. **Apoptosis Inhibition via CASP8**:  \n   Upregulation of *CFLAR* (c-FLIP) in the **CASP8 Inhibition** pathway may suppress caspase-8 activation, blocking extrinsic apoptosis. This suggests a resistance mechanism where cancer cells evade apoptosis despite *FAS* and *TNFRSF10* activation.\n\n4. **NFE2L2-Driven Survival**:  \n   *NFE2L2* (NRF2) activates *BCL2L1* (anti-apoptotic) and *NOTCH1*, promoting survival under oxidative stress. This pathway may counteract cisplatin’s cytotoxic effects, contributing to resistance.\n\n5. **Metabolic Reprogramming**:  \n   Enrichment of **Gluconeogenesis** (*GPI, TPI1, GAPDH, ENO1*) may reflect metabolic adaptation to stress, as glycolytic enzymes are repurposed for survival under genotoxic conditions.\n\n---\n\n### Implications for Therapy\n- **Combination Strategies**: Inhibiting **NFE2L2** or **CFLAR** could enhance cisplatin’s efficacy by preventing resistance.  \n- **ER Stress Modulation**: Targeting ER stress pathways (e.g., PERK/ATF4) may amplify apoptosis in cisplatin-treated cells.  \n- **TP53 Status**: Functional TP53 is critical for cisplatin response; TP53 mutations may necessitate alternative therapies.\n\n---\n\n### Next Steps & Clarification\n1. **Validate TP53 Status**: Confirm TP53 functionality in CRC cell lines to assess reliance on this pathway.  \n2. **Target NFE2L2/BCL2L1**: Test inhibitors (e.g., BCL2L1 antagonists) to reverse resistance.  \n3. **ER Stress Modulation**: Investigate PERK/ATF4 pathway activators to enhance cisplatin-induced apoptosis.  \n4. **CASP8 Re-activation**: Explore strategies to overcome *CFLAR*-mediated CASP8 inhibition.\n\n---\n\n### References\n1. PMID 15520850: TP53-mediated cell cycle arrest and apoptosis in response to DNA damage.  \n2. PMID 25652198: ATF4/CHOP pathway in ER stress-induced apoptosis.  \n3. PMID 31061454: NFE2L2-driven antioxidant response in cancer drug resistance.  \n4. PMID 28216627: Role of glycolytic enzymes in stress adaptation.",
